<p><hr align=left size=1 noshade width="100%"><p>
<h1><a name="W-section">W</a></h1>


<p><b><a name="weight">Weight</a></b> &nbsp; &nbsp;
In a <a href="glossary-N.html#neural_network_(nn)">neural network</a>, the strength of a <a href="glossary-S.html#synapse">synapse</a> (or
connection) between two <a href="glossary-N.html#neuron">neurons</a>.  Weights may be positive
(<a href="glossary-E.html#excitatory">excitatory</a>) or negative (<a href="glossary-I.html#inhibitory">inhibitory</a>).  The <a href="glossary-T.html#threshold">thresholds</a>
of a neuron are also considered weights, since they undergo
<a href="glossary-A.html#adaptation">adaptation</a> by a <a href="glossary-L.html#learning">learning</a> <a href="glossary-A.html#algorithm">algorithm</a>.  

<p><b><a name="white_noise">White Noise</a></b> &nbsp; &nbsp;
Noise that uniformly distributed in the frequency domain;
<a href="glossary-R.html#random/randomness">randomness</a> that is uniformly distributed; thus, a white noise
process with a range of 0 to 1 would yield a random number in this
range with <a href="glossary-P.html#probability">probability</a> equal for all possible values.  <a href="glossary-B.html#brown_noise/brownian_motion">Brown
noise</a> is a result of cumulatively adding white noise. 



